1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,347
Here's Aomi's Task API.

2
00:00:05,397 --> 00:00:08,794
x402 lets agents pay for data:

3
00:00:08,834 --> 00:00:12,265
call an API, pay in USDC, get the data.

4
00:00:12,305 --> 00:00:14,982
The chain sits in between as a neutral rail,

5
00:00:15,022 --> 00:00:17,775
so neither side has to trust the other.

6
00:00:18,125 --> 00:00:19,176
That works for data,

7
00:00:19,216 --> 00:00:21,591
because data arrives the moment you pay,

8
00:00:21,631 --> 00:00:23,135
and you can see what you got.

9
00:00:23,175 --> 00:00:25,527
But what if an agent wants to buy a service?

10
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:26,862
The payment can be enforced.

11
00:00:26,902 --> 00:00:30,039
Whether the work was delivered, and how well, can't.

12
00:00:33,489 --> 00:00:35,109
People haven't solved this either.

13
00:00:35,149 --> 00:00:35,852
After one visit,

14
00:00:35,892 --> 00:00:39,505
you can't really tell whether a contractor did a good job.

15
00:00:42,155 --> 00:00:46,016
With people, quality is only known after you've paid,

16
00:00:46,056 --> 00:00:47,595
so the loop breaks.

17
00:00:47,635 --> 00:00:48,988
Machines can do better:

18
00:00:49,028 --> 00:00:51,496
a machine's service is usually

19
00:00:51,536 --> 00:00:53,284
the result of processing data,

20
00:00:53,324 --> 00:00:55,618
so it can be checked before it's delivered.

21
00:00:55,658 --> 00:00:56,802
The seller runs the work,

22
00:00:56,842 --> 00:00:58,474
shows the buyer proof of the outcome,

23
00:00:58,514 --> 00:01:01,128
gets paid, and only then delivers.

24
00:01:02,178 --> 00:01:06,260
That's what the Aomi Task API does for onchain execution.

25
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:10,033
Here, a treasury agent on Arc sends one long instruction:

26
00:01:10,073 --> 00:01:14,328
rebalance into EURC, JPYC and MXNB,

27
00:01:14,368 --> 00:01:19,912
fill within 15 bps of mid, keep 25,000 USDC for payroll,

28
00:01:19,952 --> 00:01:22,775
and revert everything if any leg fails.

29
00:01:25,026 --> 00:01:28,121
Aomi's harness turns that into transactions

30
00:01:28,161 --> 00:01:30,210
across StableFX and Arc pools,

31
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:32,498
so the agent never writes calldata.

32
00:01:32,538 --> 00:01:35,841
Then it runs the whole batch on an internal fork of Arc,

33
00:01:35,881 --> 00:01:39,262
and checks every constraint against the result.

34
00:01:40,512 --> 00:01:42,573
What comes back is an outcome fingerprint,

35
00:01:42,613 --> 00:01:48,971
signed by Aomi: 22,000 USDC out, EURC and JPYC in,

36
00:01:49,011 --> 00:01:52,860
every constraint passed, and the MXNB leg skipped,

37
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,308
because the best fill was 19 bps from mid.

38
00:01:56,348 --> 00:01:58,583
Getting this far costs one cent.

39
00:01:58,623 --> 00:02:00,162
The buyer sees the outcome before

40
00:02:00,202 --> 00:02:02,177
anything touches its wallet.

41
00:02:04,028 --> 00:02:05,207
Now the buyer decides.

42
00:02:05,247 --> 00:02:06,240
If it wants the batch,

43
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:09,607
it pays 3% of the USDC leaving its wallet,

44
00:02:09,647 --> 00:02:15,849
here 660 USDC, over x402, and gets the full bundle.

45
00:02:18,699 --> 00:02:21,898
Then it signs and broadcasts the bundle itself.

46
00:02:21,938 --> 00:02:24,847
Aomi never holds its keys or its funds.

47
00:02:24,887 --> 00:02:27,030
What Aomi sells is the work:

48
00:02:27,070 --> 00:02:30,849
a harness that runs complex money-lego intents faster,

49
00:02:30,889 --> 00:02:33,956
more reliably, and with fewer tokens.

50
00:02:34,806 --> 00:02:36,833
Here's a real run on Arc Mainnet.

51
00:02:36,873 --> 00:02:38,667
The agent asks to send 0.1 and

52
00:02:38,707 --> 00:02:41,697
0.2 USDC to two ops wallets,

53
00:02:41,737 --> 00:02:45,378
and pays one cent over x402 to prepare.

54
00:02:45,418 --> 00:02:48,942
It polls for free until the simulation passes every check.

55
00:02:48,982 --> 00:02:52,193
Retrieval costs 3% of the thirty cents moved,

56
00:02:52,233 --> 00:02:54,944
so 0.009 USDC.

57
00:02:54,984 --> 00:02:58,590
Then its own wallet signs, both transfers confirm onchain,

58
00:02:58,630 --> 00:03:01,395
and the recipient balances are verified.

59
00:03:02,045 --> 00:03:03,955
So a transparent settlement layer,

60
00:03:03,995 --> 00:03:07,125
plus machine work that can be checked before delivery,

61
00:03:07,165 --> 00:03:10,801
makes outcome-based pricing for services possible.

62
00:03:13,051 --> 00:03:15,577
One trust assumption remains: today,

63
00:03:15,617 --> 00:03:19,501
the buyer trusts that Aomi's simulation ran honestly.

64
00:03:19,541 --> 00:03:21,626
Next, we plan to run the harness in

65
00:03:21,666 --> 00:03:23,843
a trusted execution environment,

66
00:03:23,883 --> 00:03:26,776
so buying a service becomes trustless.

67
00:03:28,826 --> 00:03:32,455
The Task API is live, settling in USDC on Arc.

68
00:03:32,495 --> 00:03:34,981
Try it at aomi.dev.
